1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,757
You double-click a game.

2
00:00:02,037 --> 00:00:04,690
Three seconds later, you're on the main menu.

3
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:12,376
In those three seconds, every single part inside your PC did a job, and most people couldn't name half of them.

4
00:00:12,656 --> 00:00:16,755
So let's follow that one click through the whole machine, part by part.

5
00:00:17,035 --> 00:00:24,111
By the end, you'll know what every component does, which ones actually matter for speed, and where people waste money.

6
00:00:24,861 --> 00:00:25,970
Start at the wall.

7
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:32,144
Your outlet sends out alternating current, around 120 volts in North America.

8
00:00:32,424 --> 00:00:34,775
Nothing inside your PC can use that.

9
00:00:35,055 --> 00:00:38,215
So the first part that wakes up is the power supply.

10
00:00:38,495 --> 00:00:50,579
Its whole job is translation: it turns wall power into steady, low-voltage direct current, mostly 12 volts, plus 5 and 3.3 volts for smaller parts.

11
00:00:50,859 --> 00:00:58,702
Every other component depends on it, which is why a cheap, unrated power supply is the one corner you should never cut.

12
00:00:58,982 --> 00:01:01,481
When it fails, it can take other parts with it.

13
00:01:01,761 --> 00:01:09,342
Look for an efficiency rating like 80 Plus Bronze or better, and a wattage with some headroom above what your parts draw.

14
00:01:10,092 --> 00:01:12,204
That power flows into the motherboard.

15
00:01:12,484 --> 00:01:15,940
Think of the motherboard as the city the other parts live in.

16
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:17,599
It doesn't do the thinking.

17
00:01:17,879 --> 00:01:19,319
It provides the roads.

18
00:01:19,599 --> 00:01:28,360
Copper traces connect the processor to the memory, the graphics card, and the storage, and the board decides how fast those roads can run.

19
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:34,846
It also holds a small chip with firmware called UEFI, the modern version of the BIOS.

20
00:01:35,126 --> 00:01:38,403
That's the first code that runs when you press the power button.

21
00:01:38,683 --> 00:01:43,851
It checks that everything is present, then hands control to your operating system.

22
00:01:44,601 --> 00:01:45,974
Now, back to your click.

23
00:01:46,254 --> 00:01:56,298
Your mouse sends a tiny signal through USB, and the motherboard delivers it to the brain of the system: the CPU, or central processing unit.

24
00:01:56,578 --> 00:02:02,459
The CPU runs instructions one tiny step at a time, billions of times per second.

25
00:02:02,739 --> 00:02:04,868
That's what the gigahertz number means.

26
00:02:05,148 --> 00:02:09,699
A 4 gigahertz chip ticks through about four billion cycles every second.

27
00:02:09,979 --> 00:02:16,084
Modern CPUs also have multiple cores, which are like several workers sharing one desk.

28
00:02:16,364 --> 00:02:20,703
Your click becomes a list of instructions: find the game, load it, start it.

29
00:02:20,983 --> 00:02:24,039
The CPU starts working through that list immediately.

30
00:02:24,789 --> 00:02:26,611
But the CPU has a problem.

31
00:02:26,891 --> 00:02:28,636
The game isn't anywhere near it.

32
00:02:28,916 --> 00:02:30,643
It's sitting on your storage drive.

33
00:02:30,923 --> 00:02:33,783
Storage is the long-term memory of your PC.

34
00:02:34,063 --> 00:02:39,683
It keeps everything when the power is off: your operating system, your games, your photos.

35
00:02:39,963 --> 00:02:41,464
There are two main kinds.

36
00:02:41,744 --> 00:02:49,009
A hard drive stores data on spinning magnetic platters, read by an arm that physically moves into place.

37
00:02:49,289 --> 00:02:56,147
It's cheap and roomy, but slow, usually around 150 to 200 megabytes per second.

38
00:02:56,427 --> 00:02:59,590
A solid state drive has no moving parts at all.

39
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:02,329
It stores data in flash memory chips.

40
00:03:02,609 --> 00:03:10,564
A good NVMe SSD, the kind that slots straight into the motherboard, can read thousands of megabytes per second.

41
00:03:10,844 --> 00:03:18,668
That's why the single biggest upgrade for an old, slow PC is usually swapping a hard drive for an SSD.

42
00:03:19,418 --> 00:03:22,237
So the game files start flowing off the SSD.

43
00:03:22,517 --> 00:03:24,624
But they don't go straight to the CPU.

44
00:03:24,904 --> 00:03:30,995
Storage, even fast storage, is still far too slow for the processor to work from directly.

45
00:03:31,275 --> 00:03:33,308
So the files land in RAM.

46
00:03:33,588 --> 00:03:40,177
RAM is short-term memory: a fast workspace the CPU can grab from in nanoseconds.

47
00:03:40,457 --> 00:03:41,852
Here's the key difference.

48
00:03:42,132 --> 00:03:45,558
RAM forgets everything the moment you cut the power.

49
00:03:45,838 --> 00:03:47,167
Storage remembers.

50
00:03:47,447 --> 00:03:49,729
RAM is the desk you're working at.

51
00:03:50,009 --> 00:03:52,871
Storage is the filing cabinet across the room.

52
00:03:53,151 --> 00:03:59,529
More RAM means a bigger desk, so you can keep more open at once without walking back to the cabinet.

53
00:03:59,809 --> 00:04:04,952
But once your desk is big enough for what you do, adding more doesn't make anything faster.

54
00:04:05,232 --> 00:04:10,744
For most gaming right now, 16 to 32 gigabytes is the comfortable range.

55
00:04:11,024 --> 00:04:14,779
Past that, you're usually paying for desk space you'll never use.

56
00:04:15,529 --> 00:04:18,817
Even RAM is too slow for the CPU's taste.

57
00:04:19,097 --> 00:04:25,514
So the processor keeps its own tiny, extremely fast memory built right into the chip, called cache.

58
00:04:25,794 --> 00:04:34,004
It's measured in megabytes, not gigabytes, but it's so close to the cores that the CPU checks it first for anything it needs.

59
00:04:34,284 --> 00:04:36,493
Cache, then RAM, then storage.

60
00:04:36,773 --> 00:04:40,438
Each step is bigger, cheaper, and slower than the one before.

61
00:04:41,188 --> 00:04:48,496
With the game loaded, it's time to draw the main menu, and this is where the CPU hands off to a specialist.

62
00:04:48,776 --> 00:04:53,444
The GPU, or graphics processing unit, lives on your graphics card.

63
00:04:53,724 --> 00:04:56,860
A CPU has a handful of very smart cores.

64
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:59,804
A GPU has thousands of simpler ones.

65
00:05:00,084 --> 00:05:05,281
That sounds like a downgrade, but drawing an image is a perfect job for a crowd.

66
00:05:05,561 --> 00:05:12,450
Your screen has millions of pixels, and most of them can be calculated at the same time, independently.

67
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:19,166
So the GPU splits the frame into a massive number of small tasks and runs them all in parallel.

68
00:05:19,446 --> 00:05:26,255
The graphics card also has its own memory, called VRAM, where it keeps textures and models close at hand.

69
00:05:26,535 --> 00:05:32,269
In games, the GPU usually matters more for frame rate than the CPU does.

70
00:05:32,549 --> 00:05:36,274
That's why it's often the most expensive part in a gaming build.

71
00:05:37,024 --> 00:05:38,783
All that work produces heat.

72
00:05:39,063 --> 00:05:48,022
A CPU or GPU under load can turn well over a hundred watts of electricity into heat, in a space smaller than a cracker.

73
00:05:48,302 --> 00:05:49,878
So the next part is cooling.

74
00:05:50,158 --> 00:05:57,116
A cooler sits on the CPU, with a thin layer of thermal paste filling the microscopic gaps between them.

75
00:05:57,396 --> 00:06:02,133
Heat moves into the cooler's metal fins or liquid, and fans push it away.

76
00:06:02,413 --> 00:06:05,251
Your graphics card has its own cooler built in.

77
00:06:05,531 --> 00:06:08,632
And the case fans move fresh air through the whole system.

78
00:06:08,912 --> 00:06:13,462
If cooling falls behind, the chips protect themselves by slowing down.

79
00:06:13,742 --> 00:06:19,693
That's called thermal throttling, and it's a common hidden reason a PC feels slower in summer.

80
00:06:20,443 --> 00:06:21,638
Finally, the case.

81
00:06:21,918 --> 00:06:23,395
It's not just a box.

82
00:06:23,675 --> 00:06:33,112
It sets the airflow path, protects the parts, and decides what fits inside: how long a graphics card, how tall a cooler, how many drives.

83
00:06:33,862 --> 00:06:35,475
So let's replay that click.

84
00:06:35,755 --> 00:06:37,851
The power supply feeds clean power.

85
00:06:38,131 --> 00:06:40,014
The motherboard connects everything.

86
00:06:40,294 --> 00:06:43,839
The CPU reads your click and starts the instructions.

87
00:06:44,119 --> 00:06:46,695
The SSD hands over the game files.

88
00:06:46,975 --> 00:06:48,777
RAM holds them close.

89
00:06:49,057 --> 00:06:51,443
Cache feeds the CPU even faster.

90
00:06:51,723 --> 00:06:56,054
The GPU draws the menu across millions of pixels at once.

91
00:06:56,334 --> 00:06:58,674
And the coolers keep it all from overheating.

92
00:06:58,954 --> 00:07:01,770
Three seconds, eight parts, all working together.

93
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:09,298
If your PC feels slow, the fix depends on which part is the bottleneck, and most people guess wrong.

94
00:07:09,578 --> 00:07:16,497
That's the next video: what actually makes a PC slow, and why more RAM usually isn't the answer.

95
00:07:16,777 --> 00:07:19,544
If this helped, subscribe, and I'll see you there.

